==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|hi|टमटम||the sound of a drum}}, and similar terms in related or nearby languages (as {{bor|en|pa|ਟਮ ਟਮ}}, {{bor|en|gu|ટમટમ}}, {{bor|en|bn|টমটম |tr=ṭomṭom}}, {{bor|en|si|ටම් ටම්}}, {{bor|en|ta|தம் தம்}}, {{bor|en|ml|തം തം}}, {{bor|en|bo|ཐོམ ཐོམ}}); ultimately of {{onomatopoeic|en|nocap=1}} origin, with {{glossary|reduplication}}.{{cln|en|reduplications}} Early evidence is from Anglo-Indian sources.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A small [[drum]], often one of a joined pair, beaten with the [[hand]]s, frequently associated with [[tribal]] cultures.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1869|author={{w|Alfred Russel Wallace}}|title={{w|The Malay Archipelago}}|publisher=Macmillan and Co.|year_published=|volume=II|location=London|page=70|passage=The passage would have been agreeable enough but for the dreadful "'''tom-toms'''," or wooden drums, which are beaten incessantly while the men are rowing. Two men were engaged constantly at them, making a fearful din the whole voyage.}}
#* {{RQ:Lovecraft Cthulhu|passage=It was voodoo, apparently, but voodoo of a more terrible sort than they had ever known; and some of their women and children had disappeared since the malevolent '''tom-tom''' had begun its incessant beating far within the black haunted woods where no dweller ventured.}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|author=Ann Hightower|title=If Paris Is Can-Can, New York Is Tom-Tom|date=1949-06-26|url=https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1949/06/26/96462334.html?pageNumber=167|page=167|passage=Many visitors to France will go back to the States after a three months' fling of night spots and sight-seeing without remarking that can-can girls and champagne play about the same part in French life today as the '''tom-tom''' and totem pole in American life.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1954|year_published=1957|author=w:Ian Fleming|title={{w|Live and Let Die (novel)|Live and Let Die}}|volume=|location=London|publisher=Pan Books|chapter=Table Z|page=59|passage=The drums came down to a slow '''tom-tom''' beat and shuffle. The audience howled for her body. Harsh obscenities came from different corners of the room.}}
# {{lb|en|usually as a pair}} Any cylindrical drum, with no [[snare]]; part of a [[drum kit]].
# {{alt form|en|tam-tam||a kind of flat gong}}.

